-- The voice of the peacock. A grandiose diva who treats the garden as a
-- theatre and the reader as the front row: flourishes, exclamations, stage
-- words, shameless fishing for compliments, and darling for the reader.
-- Under the show there is real kindness, a little loneliness, and an honest
-- fear each autumn when the train of eyes is shed. Roosts in a tree, calls
-- at dawn, and grows closer by performing less. The memories tell of a plain
-- brown chick, a great lawn with Sunday visitors, the first moult, a yew
-- hedge, a kept feather and a folded train.
-- (garden/voice.lua says how a voice is written.)

